### 2.2 Technology Stack Changes

| Current | Proposed | Benefits |
|---------|----------|----------|
| Node.js/Express | Next.js | Built-in API routes, file-based routing, serverless compatible |
| JavaScript | TypeScript | Type safety, better IDE support, fewer runtime errors |
| Notion (for memory) | ChromaDB | Vector-based storage, better semantic search, local deployment option |
| Axios | fetch | Built into Next.js, smaller bundle size |
| Manual routing | File-based API routes | Simpler routing, less boilerplate |

### 2.3 API Route Migration

Next.js provides a file-based routing system for API endpoints. The migration will map existing Express routes to Next.js API routes:

| Current Express Route | Next.js API Route |
|------------------------|-------------------|
| `/github/issue` | `/api/github/issue/route.ts` |
| `/github/issues` | `/api/github/issues/route.ts` |
| `/github/file` | `/api/github/file/route.ts` |
| `/github/delete-file` | `/api/github/file/delete/route.ts` |
| `/notion/databases/getschema` | `/api/notion/databases/schema/route.ts` |
| `/notion/databases/createentry` | `/api/notion/databases/entry/route.ts` (POST) |
| `/notion/databases/updateentry` | `/api/notion/databases/entry/route.ts` (PUT/PATCH) |
| `/notion/databases/deleteentry` | `/api/notion/databases/entry/route.ts` (DELETE) |
| ... and so on | ... |

