## 5. ChromaDB Integration

### 5.1 ChromaDB Overview

ChromaDB is an open-source embedding database that will replace the current Notion-based memory system with:

- Vector-based storage for semantic search
- Better query capabilities 
- Local deployment option
- Improved performance for memory-related operations

### 5.2 ChromaDB Collections Design

Based on your existing memory structure, we'll create the following collections:

1. **Memory Collection** - Direct replacement for GPT_MEMORY_DB
   - Key (string) - Unique identifier (previously Title in Notion)
   - Value (text) - The memory content
   - Tags (array) - Associated tags for filtering
   - LastUpdated (date) - Timestamp of last update
   - Pinned (boolean) - Whether the memory is pinned
   - Embedding (vector) - Vector representation of the memory content

### 5.3 ChromaDB Client Implementation

```typescript
// lib/external/chroma/client.ts
import { ChromaClient, Collection } from 'chromadb';

export class ChromaDBClient {
  private client: ChromaClient;
  private memoryCollection: Collection;

  constructor() {
    this.client = new ChromaClient({
      path: process.env.CHROMA_API_URL || 'http://localhost:8000'
    });
  }

  async initialize() {
    // Get or create the memory collection
    this.memoryCollection = await this.client.getOrCreateCollection({
      name: "memory",
      metadata: { 
        description: "GPT memory storage" 
      }
    });
  }

  async createMemory(key: string, value: string, metadata: any = {}) {
    await this.memoryCollection.add({
      ids: [key],
      documents: [value],
      metadatas: [{
        ...metadata,
        lastUpdated: new Date().toISOString()
      }]
    });

    return { id: key, created: true };
  }

  async getMemory(key: string) {
    const result = await this.memoryCollection.get({
      ids: [key]
    });

    if (result.ids.length === 0) {
      return null;
    }

    return {
      id: result.ids[0],
      value: result.documents[0],
      metadata: result.metadatas[0]
    };
  }

  async updateMemory(key: string, value: string, metadata: any = {}) {
    await this.memoryCollection.update({
      ids: [key],
      documents: [value],
      metadatas: [{
        ...metadata,
        lastUpdated: new Date().toISOString()
      }]
    });

    return { id: key, updated: true };
  }

  async deleteMemory(key: string) {
    await this.memoryCollection.delete({
      ids: [key]
    });

    return { id: key, deleted: true };
  }

  async listMemories(filters: any = {}) {
    // Convert filters to ChromaDB format
    const chromaFilters = this.convertFilters(filters);

    const result = await this.memoryCollection.get({
      where: chromaFilters
    });

    return result.ids.map((id, index) => ({
      id,
      value: result.documents[index],
      metadata: result.metadatas[index]
    }));
  }

  async searchMemories(query: string, limit: number = 10) {
    const result = await this.memoryCollection.query({
      queryTexts: [query],
      nResults: limit
    });

    return result.ids[0].map((id, index) => ({
      id,
      value: result.documents[0][index],
      metadata: result.metadatas[0][index],
      distance: result.distances[0][index]
    }));
  }

  private convertFilters(filters: any) {
    // Convert from Notion filter format to ChromaDB filter format
    // Implementation would depend on your specific filtering needs
    return filters;
  }
}

export const chromaClient = new ChromaDBClient();
```

### 5.4 Migration Utility for Notion to ChromaDB

```typescript
// lib/external/chroma/migration.ts
import { notionClient } from '../notion/api';
import { chromaClient } from './client';

export async function migrateNotionMemoriesToChroma() {
  // Initialize ChromaDB client
  await chromaClient.initialize();

  // Fetch all memories from Notion
  const memoryDbId = process.env.GPT_MEMORY_DB;
  if (!memoryDbId) {
    throw new Error('GPT_MEMORY_DB environment variable not set');
  }

  const { results } = await notionClient.queryDatabase(memoryDbId);

  console.log(`Migrating ${results.length} memories from Notion to ChromaDB...`);

  // Process each memory
  for (const page of results) {
    // Extract data from Notion
    const key = page.properties.Title?.title?.[0]?.text?.content || page.id;
    const value = page.properties.Value?.rich_text?.[0]?.text?.content || '';
    const tags = page.properties.Tags?.multi_select?.map(tag => tag.name) || [];
    const lastUpdated = page.properties.LastUpdated?.date?.start || new Date().toISOString();
    const pinned = page.properties.Pinned?.checkbox || false;

    // Create in ChromaDB
    try {
      await chromaClient.createMemory(key, value, {
        tags,
        lastUpdated,
        pinned
      });
      console.log(`Migrated memory: ${key}`);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(`Failed to migrate memory ${key}:`, error);
    }
  }

  console.log('Migration complete!');
}
```

### 5.5 API Route for ChromaDB Memory

```typescript
// app/api/memory/route.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';
import { z } from 'zod';
import { chromaClient } from '@/lib/external/chroma/client';

// Input validation schemas
const CreateSchema = z.object({
  key: z.string(),
  value: z.string(),
  tags: z.array(z.string()).optional(),
  pinned: z.boolean().optional()
});

export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const body = await request.json();

    // Validate input
    const result = CreateSchema.safeParse(body);
    if (!result.success) {
      return NextResponse.json(
        { error: 'Invalid request data', details: result.error },
        { status: 400 }
      );
    }

    const { key, value, tags, pinned } = result.data;

    // Initialize ChromaDB if needed
    await chromaClient.initialize();

    // Create memory
    const memory = await chromaClient.createMemory(key, value, {
      tags,
      pinned,
      lastUpdated: new Date().toISOString()
    });

    return NextResponse.json({
      success: true,
      created: true,
      id: memory.id
    });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error creating memory:', error);
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: 'Failed to create memory', message: error.message },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}

export async function GET(request: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const { searchParams } = new URL(request.url);
    const key = searchParams.get('key');
    const query = searchParams.get('query');

    // Initialize ChromaDB if needed
    await chromaClient.initialize();

    // If key is provided, get specific memory
    if (key) {
      const memory = await chromaClient.getMemory(key);

      if (!memory) {
        return NextResponse.json(
          { error: 'Memory not found' },
          { status: 404 }
        );
      }

      return NextResponse.json(memory);
    }

    // If query is provided, search memories
    if (query) {
      const limit = parseInt(searchParams.get('limit') || '10', 10);
      const memories = await chromaClient.searchMemories(query, limit);
      return NextResponse.json(memories);
    }

    // Otherwise, list all memories
    const memories = await chromaClient.listMemories();
    return NextResponse.json(memories);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error retrieving memories:', error);
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: 'Failed to retrieve memories', message: error.message },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}

// PUT and DELETE methods would be implemented similarly
```

## 6. Summary and Next Steps

This migration plan outlines a comprehensive strategy for transitioning your Node.js/Express application to a Next.js/TypeScript architecture with ChromaDB integration. The approach maintains the same functionality while introducing improved type safety, better architecture, and enhanced memory capabilities.

### Key Benefits

1. **Improved Developer Experience**: TypeScript provides better type safety and IDE support
2. **Enhanced Performance**: Next.js optimizations and ChromaDB for faster memory operations
3. **Better Maintainability**: Clearer code organization with file-based routing
4. **Future-Proof Architecture**: Serverless-compatible design for flexible deployment options
5. **Improved Search Capabilities**: Vector-based search with ChromaDB for better semantic matching

### Next Steps

1. Initialize the Next.js project and set up the basic structure
2. Implement core utility libraries and type definitions
3. Migrate API clients to TypeScript
4. Implement API routes for each endpoint
5. Set up ChromaDB and create migration utilities
6. Test and validate the new system
7. Deploy and monitor the migration

