## 5. Data Validation

### Schema-Level Validation

MongoDB and Mongoose already provide schema-level validation. However, for more complex validation, you can add custom validators:

```typescript
// Enhanced validation in User Schema
const UserSchema = new Schema<IUser>({
  email: {
    type: String,
    required: [true, 'Email is required'],
    unique: true,
    validate: {
      validator: function(v: string) {
        return /^[\w-\.]+@([\w-]+\.)+[\w-]{2,4}$/.test(v);
      },
      message: props => `${props.value} is not a valid email!`
    }
  },
  // ... other fields
});
```

### API-Level Validation

For API routes, implement validation using a library like Zod:

1. Install Zod:

```bash
npm install zod
```

2. Create validators (`/lib/validators/users.ts`):

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

export const createUserSchema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  name: z.string().min(2).max(100),
  avatar: z.string().url().optional(),
});

export const updateUserSchema = createUserSchema.partial();

export type CreateUserInput = z.infer<typeof createUserSchema>;
export type UpdateUserInput = z.infer<typeof updateUserSchema>;
```

3. Use in API routes:

```typescript
// /app/api/users/route.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';
import { createUserSchema } from '@/lib/validators/users';
import { createUser } from '@/lib/db/queries/users';

export async function POST(req: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const body = await req.json();

    // Validate input
    const result = createUserSchema.safeParse(body);
    if (!result.success) {
      return NextResponse.json(
        { error: 'Validation error', details: result.error.format() },
        { status: 400 }
      );
    }

    // Create user
    const user = await createUser(result.data);
    return NextResponse.json(user);
  } catch (error: any) {
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: error.message || 'Failed to create user' },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```

## 6. Performance Optimization with Indexes

### Creating Indexes

Define indexes in your schema definitions:

```typescript
// User Schema with index
const UserSchema = new Schema<IUser>(
  {
    email: {
      type: String,
      required: true,
      unique: true, // This creates an index automatically
    },
    // ... other fields
  }
);

// Create a compound index
UserSchema.index({ createdAt: -1, name: 1 });
