11. [Accessibility Considerations](#accessibility-considerations)
12. [Mobile Responsiveness](#mobile-responsiveness)
13. [Final Integration in Your Project](#final-integration-in-your-project)

## Introduction

This guide will walk you through integrating TipTap, a headless rich text editor based on ProseMirror, into your Next.js application. TipTap provides a flexible foundation for creating custom rich text editing experiences while maintaining good performance and extensibility.

TipTap is ideal for your application which includes blog posts, lessons, and other content creation needs because:

- It's framework-agnostic but works exceptionally well with React
- It offers a modular extension system
- It supports collaborative editing
- It generates clean, semantic HTML output
- It's highly customizable for your specific needs

Let's begin implementing TipTap in your Next.js project.

## Installation and Setup

First, install the core TipTap packages and necessary extensions. For your Next.js application, we'll use TipTap version 2.x which offers better TypeScript support and a more modular approach.

```bash
npm install @tiptap/react @tiptap/pm @tiptap/extension-image @tiptap/extension-link @tiptap/extension-code-block @tiptap/extension-table @tiptap/extension-table-row @tiptap/extension-table-cell @tiptap/extension-table-header
npm install @tiptap/starter-kit @tiptap/extension-placeholder
```

The `@tiptap/starter-kit` includes common extensions like bold, italic, headings, and lists, which saves us from installing them individually.

Next, create the base folder structure for your editor components:

```
/components/
  /editor/
    index.ts           # Export all editor components
    Editor.tsx         # Main editor component
    MenuBar.tsx        # Formatting toolbar 
    BubbleMenu.tsx     # Context menu that appears on selection
    FloatingMenu.tsx   # Menu that appears on empty lines
    extensions/        # Custom extensions
      index.ts
    plugins/           # Custom plugins
      index.ts
```

## Creating a Basic Editor Component

Let's create the main `Editor.tsx` component which will serve as the foundation for your rich text editor.

```tsx
// components/editor/Editor.tsx
import { useEditor, EditorContent } from '@tiptap/react';
import StarterKit from '@tiptap/starter-kit';
import Placeholder from '@tiptap/extension-placeholder';
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import MenuBar from './MenuBar';

interface EditorProps {
  initialContent?: string;
  placeholder?: string;
  onChange?: (html: string) => void;
  editable?: boolean;
}

export default function Editor({
  initialContent = '<p></p>',
  placeholder = 'Start writing...',
  onChange = () => {},
  editable = true,
}: EditorProps) {
  const [isMounted, setIsMounted] = useState(false);

  // Initialize the editor
  const editor = useEditor({
    extensions: [
      StarterKit,
      Placeholder.configure({
        placeholder,
      }),
    ],
    content: initialContent,
    editable,
    onUpdate: ({ editor }) => {
      // Call the onChange callback with new content
      onChange(editor.getHTML());
    },
  });

  // Handle client-side mounting to avoid hydration issues
  useEffect(() => {
    setIsMounted(true);
  }, []);

  // Don't render the editor on the server
  if (!isMounted) {
    return null;
  }

  return (
    <div className="editor-container">
      {editor && editable && <MenuBar editor={editor} />}
      <EditorContent editor={editor} className="prose max-w-none" />
    </div>
  );
}
```

