## Caching and Optimization Strategies

### 1. Cloudflare Caching Rules

Setup caching rules in your Cloudflare dashboard:

1. Go to Caching > Configuration
2. Create a cache rule:
   - For static assets (images, CSS, JS): Set TTL to 7 days
   - For API responses: Set based on data volatility (maybe 1 hour)

### 2. Next.js Optimization

Update `next.config.mjs` with optimization settings:

```javascript
// next.config.mjs
const nextConfig = {
  reactStrictMode: true,
  swcMinify: true,
  images: {
    domains: ['yourdomain.com'],
    formats: ['image/avif', 'image/webp'],
  },
  compiler: {
    removeConsole: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production',
  },
  experimental: {
    optimizeCss: true,
    serverComponentsExternalPackages: ['mongoose'],
  },
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/(.*)',
        headers: [
          {
            key: 'Cache-Control',
            value: 'public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=86400',
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        source: '/api/(.*)',
        headers: [
          {
            key: 'Cache-Control',
            value: 'no-store, max-age=0',
          },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};

export default nextConfig;
```

### 3. Implementing Service Worker

Create a service worker for client-side caching at `public/sw.js`:

```javascript
// public/sw.js
const CACHE_NAME = 'next-app-cache-v1';
const urlsToCache = [
  '/',
  '/offline',
  '/styles/globals.css',
  // Add other static assets
];

self.addEventListener('install', event => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches.open(CACHE_NAME)
      .then(cache => cache.addAll(urlsToCache))
  );
});

self.addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  // Network first, fall back to cache
  event.respondWith(
    fetch(event.request)
      .catch(() => caches.match(event.request))
  );
});

// Clear old caches on activate
self.addEventListener('activate', event => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches.keys().then(cacheNames => {
      return Promise.all(
        cacheNames.filter(cacheName => {
          return cacheName !== CACHE_NAME;
        }).map(cacheName => {
          return caches.delete(cacheName);
        })
      );
    })
  );
});
```

Register the service worker in `pages/_app.js`:

```javascript
// Register service worker
useEffect(() => {
  if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
    navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js').catch(err => {
      console.error('Service worker registration failed:', err);
    });
  }
}, []);
```

## SSL/TLS Certificate Setup

### 1. Cloudflare SSL/TLS Configuration

SSL/TLS is automatically managed by Cloudflare when using Tunnels. To configure:

1. Navigate to SSL/TLS > Overview in your Cloudflare dashboard
2. Set the encryption mode to "Full (strict)"
3. Enable "Always Use HTTPS" under Edge Certificates
4. Optional: Enable TLS 1.3 for improved security and performance

### 2. Certificate Monitoring

Set up monitoring for certificate expiration:

```powershell
# certificate-monitor.ps1
$domain = "yourdomain.com"
$result = Invoke-RestMethod -Uri "https://cloudflare-dns.com/dns-query?name=$domain&type=CERT" -Headers @{"accept"="application/dns-json"}

$cert = $result.Answer | Where-Object { $_.type -eq 41 }
if ($cert) {
    $expiryDate = [DateTime]::Parse($cert.data.split(" ")[4])
    $daysRemaining = ($expiryDate - [DateTime]::Now).Days

    if ($daysRemaining -lt 30) {
        # Send alert
        Send-MailMessage -To "admin@yourdomain.com" -From "alerts@yourdomain.com" -Subject "Certificate Expiry Warning" -Body "Certificate for $domain expires in $daysRemaining days"
    }
}
```

Schedule this script to run weekly using Task Scheduler.

## Security Headers and Best Practices

### 1. Security Headers Configuration

Add security headers in `next.config.mjs`:

```javascript
// In next.config.mjs, extend the headers() function:
async headers() {
  return [
    {
      source: '/(.*)',
      headers: [
        {
          key: 'X-DNS-Prefetch-Control',
          value: 'on'
        },
        {
          key: 'Strict-Transport-Security',
          value: 'max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload'
        },
        {
          key: 'X-XSS-Protection',
          value: '1; mode=block'
        },
        {
          key: 'X-Frame-Options',
          value: 'SAMEORIGIN'
        },
        {
          key: 'X-Content-Type-Options',
          value: 'nosniff'
        },
        {
          key: 'Referrer-Policy',
          value: 'strict-origin-when-cross-origin'
        },
        {
          key: 'Content-Security-Policy',
          value: `default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' ${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL}`
        }
      ]
    }
  ];
}
```

### 2. Rate Limiting with Cloudflare

Configure rate limiting in the Cloudflare dashboard:

1. Navigate to Security > WAF > Rate limiting rules
2. Create a rule to limit requests to sensitive endpoints:
   - For login: 10 requests per minute
   - For API endpoints: 60 requests per minute

### 3. Regular Security Scanning

Set up a security scanning script:

```powershell
# security-scan.ps1
# Install required modules if not present
if (-not (Get-Module -ListAvailable -Name Pester)) {
    Install-Module -Name Pester -Force -SkipPublisherCheck
}

# Define security tests
Describe "Security Tests" {
    It "Verifies SSL configuration" {
        $result = Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://www.ssllabs.com/ssltest/analyze.html?d=yourdomain.com&latest" -UseBasicParsing
        $result.Content | Should -Match "A+"
    }

    It "Checks security headers" {
        $response = Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://yourdomain.com" -UseBasicParsing
        $response.Headers["Strict-Transport-Security"] | Should -Not -BeNullOrEmpty
        $response.Headers["X-XSS-Protection"] | Should -Not -BeNullOrEmpty
        $response.Headers["X-Frame-Options"] | Should -Not -BeNullOrEmpty
        $response.Headers["X-Content-Type-Options"] | Should -Not -BeNullOrEmpty
    }
}

# Run the tests
Invoke-Pester
```

## Monitoring and Logging Strategies

### 1. Setting Up Application Logging

Create a unified logging module at `lib/logger.js`:

```javascript
// lib/logger.js
import pino from 'pino';

const logLevel = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' ? 'info' : 'debug';

// Create logger instance
const logger = pino({
  level: logLevel,
  transport: {
    target: 'pino-pretty',
    options: {
      colorize: true,
      translateTime: 'SYS:standard',
    },
  },
});

// Create a middleware for logging API requests
export const loggerMiddleware = (req, res, next) => {
  logger.info({
    method: req.method,
    url: req.url,
    ip: req.headers['x-forwarded-for'] || req.socket.remoteAddress,
    userAgent: req.headers['user-agent'],
  }, 'Request received');

  // Log response on completion
  res.on('finish', () => {
    logger.info({
      method: req.method,
      url: req.url,
      statusCode: res.statusCode,
      responseTime: Date.now() - req._startTime,
    }, 'Response sent');
  });

  if (next) next();
};

export default logger;
```

### 2. Setting Up Cloudflare Analytics

1. Enable Cloudflare Analytics in your dashboard
2. Set up additional Analytics for specific metrics:
   - Go to Analytics > Traffic
   - Configure options for bot detection, performance metrics
   - Set up alerts for anomalies

### 3. Monitoring Script for Server Health

Create a monitoring script at `scripts/monitor-health.ps1`:

```powershell
# monitor-health.ps1
$services = @("cloudflared", "MongoDB")
$endpoints = @{
    "API" = "http://localhost:3000/api/health";
    "Web" = "http://localhost:3000";
}
$logFile = "C:\logs\health-check.log"

# Check Windows services
foreach ($service in $services) {
    $status = (Get-Service -Name $service -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).Status
    if ($status -ne "Running") {
        $message = "$(Get-Date) - WARNING: Service $service is not running (Status: $status)"
        Add-Content -Path $logFile -Value $message
        # Send alert (email, SMS, etc.)
    }
}

# Check endpoints
foreach ($endpoint in $endpoints.GetEnumerator()) {
    try {
        $response = Invoke-WebRequest -Uri $endpoint.Value -UseBasicParsing
        if ($response.StatusCode -ne 200) {
            $message = "$(Get-Date) - WARNING: Endpoint $($endpoint.Key) returned status $($response.StatusCode)"
            Add-Content -Path $logFile -Value $message
            # Send alert
        }
    } catch {
        $message = "$(Get-Date) - ERROR: Endpoint $($endpoint.Key) is unreachable: $_"
        Add-Content -Path $logFile -Value $message
        # Send alert
    }
}

# Check disk space
$disk = Get-WmiObject -Class Win32_LogicalDisk -Filter "DeviceID='C:'"
$freeSpaceGB = [math]::Round($disk.FreeSpace / 1GB, 2)
$freeSpacePercent = [math]::Round(($disk.FreeSpace / $disk.Size) * 100, 2)

if ($freeSpacePercent -lt 20) {
    $message = "$(Get-Date) - WARNING: Low disk space - $freeSpaceGB GB ($freeSpacePercent%) free"
    Add-Content -Path $logFile -Value $message
    # Send alert
}
```

## Backup and Disaster Recovery

### 1. Automated Database Backup Script

Create a backup script at `scripts/backup-db.ps1`:

```powershell
# backup-db.ps1
param (
    [string]$BackupDir = "C:\backups",
    [int]$RetentionDays = 7
)

$timestamp = Get-Date -Format "yyyyMMdd-HHmmss"
$backupFile = Join-Path $BackupDir "mongodb-$timestamp.archive"

# Create backup directory if it doesn't exist
if (-not (Test-Path $BackupDir)) {
    New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $BackupDir
}

# Run MongoDB backup
mongodump --archive=$backupFile --gzip --db=your_database_name

# Clean up old backups
Get-ChildItem -Path $BackupDir -Filter "mongodb-*.archive" | 
    Where-Object { $_.LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-$RetentionDays) } | 
    Remove-Item -Force

# Log the backup
Add-Content -Path "$BackupDir\backup-log.txt" -Value "$(Get-Date) - Backup completed: $backupFile"

# Optional: Upload to cloud storage
# az storage blob upload --account-name yourstorageaccount --container-name backups --name $backupFile --file $backupFile
```

### 2. Application Configuration Backup

Create a script to back up application configs at `scripts/backup-config.ps1`:

```powershell
# backup-config.ps1
param (
    [string]$ConfigDir = "C:\app\config",
    [string]$BackupDir = "C:\backups\config"
)

$timestamp = Get-Date -Format "yyyyMMdd-HHmmss"
$backupFile = Join-Path $BackupDir "config-$timestamp.zip"

# Create backup directory if it doesn't exist
if (-not (Test-Path $BackupDir)) {
    New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $BackupDir -Force
}

# Create backup archive
Compress-Archive -Path "$ConfigDir\*" -DestinationPath $backupFile

# Log the backup
Add-Content -Path "$BackupDir\backup-log.txt" -Value "$(Get-Date) - Config backup completed: $backupFile"
```

### 3. Disaster Recovery Plan Documentation

Create a disaster recovery document at `docs/disaster-recovery.md`:

```markdown
# Disaster Recovery Procedures

## Recovery Scenarios

### 1. Database Corruption

1. Stop the application: `pm2 stop nextjs-app`
2. Restore from most recent backup:
   ```
   mongorestore --archive=C:\backups\mongodb-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.archive --gzip
   ```
3. Restart the application: `pm2 start nextjs-app`

