 Yes, customizing the import alias for your Next.js migration project would be beneficial! The default `@/*` alias in Next.js is useful, but you can definitely customize it to better match your project structure or preferences.

Customizing import aliases can help:

1. Make imports more intuitive and meaningful
2. Create shorter import paths
3. Group related modules logically
4. Make the transition from Express easier if you maintain similar patterns

Here's how you can customize import aliases in your Next.js project:

```javascript
// next.config.js
const path = require('path');

/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  reactStrictMode: true,
  experimental: {
    turbo: true,
  },
  webpack: (config) => {
    config.resolve.alias = {
      ...config.resolve.alias,
      '@lib': path.resolve(__dirname, './src/lib'),
      '@api': path.resolve(__dirname, './src/app/api'),
      '@components': path.resolve(__dirname, './src/components'),
      '@utils': path.resolve(__dirname, './src/lib/utils'),
      '@chroma': path.resolve(__dirname, './src/lib/chroma'),
      '@notion': path.resolve(__dirname, './src/lib/notion'),
      '@github': path.resolve(__dirname, './src/lib/github'),
      '@types': path.resolve(__dirname, './src/types'),
    };
    return config;
  },
};

module.exports = nextConfig;
```

You'll also want to update your `tsconfig.json` to match these paths so TypeScript is aware of them:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"],
      "@lib/*": ["./src/lib/*"],
      "@api/*": ["./src/app/api/*"],
      "@components/*": ["./src/components/*"],
      "@utils/*": ["./src/lib/utils/*"],
      "@chroma/*": ["./src/lib/chroma/*"],
      "@notion/*": ["./src/lib/notion/*"],
      "@github/*": ["./src/lib/github/*"],
      "@types/*": ["./src/types/*"]
    }
  }
}
```

This approach would allow you to use imports like:

```typescript
// Instead of
import { getChromaClient } from '@/lib/chroma/client';
import { notionClient } from '@/lib/notion/client';

// You could use
import { getChromaClient } from '@chroma/client';
import { notionClient } from '@notion/client';
```

For your specific migration project, I would recommend aliases that mirror your current structure somewhat to make the migration more straightforward. This can help reduce cognitive load during the transition since developers would be able to more easily find and reference modules they're familiar with.

