 # Explanation of types/index.ts

This TypeScript file defines common interfaces used throughout your application. Having these types centralized makes your codebase more maintainable and enforces consistent data structures. Let me explain each interface:

## User Interface

```typescript
export interface User {
  id: string;
  name?: string;
  email: string;
  image?: string;
  role: 'user' | 'admin';
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}
```

- Defines the structure for user data in your application
- `id` and `email` are required fields
- `name` and `image` are optional (indicated by the `?` modifier)
- `role` must be either 'user' or 'admin' (union type)
- `createdAt` and `updatedAt` track when the user was created and last modified
- This type would be used for user authentication, profiles, and authorization checks

## SEO Props Interface

```typescript
export interface SEOProps {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  keywords?: string[];
  ogImage?: string;
  canonical?: string;
}
```

- Used for managing SEO metadata for your web pages
- `title` and `description` are required for all pages
- `keywords`, `ogImage` (Open Graph image for social sharing), and `canonical` URL are optional
- This type would be used with a SEO component that adds appropriate meta tags to your pages
- Helps maintain consistent SEO practices across your application

## API Response Interface

```typescript
export interface ApiResponse<T> {
  success: boolean;
  data?: T;
  error?: string;
  message?: string;
}
```

- Generic interface for standardizing API responses
- Uses TypeScript generics (`<T>`) to allow different data types in the response
- `success` indicates whether the API request was successful
- `data` holds the response data (of type T) when successful
- `error` contains error information when the request fails
- `message` provides additional context (could be used for both success and error cases)
- Creates a consistent response format across all API endpoints

## Pagination Interfaces

```typescript
export interface PaginationParams {
  page: number;
  limit: number;
  sort?: string;
  order?: 'asc' | 'desc';
}

export interface PaginatedResult<T> {
  items: T[];
  total: number;
  page: number;
  limit: number;
  pages: number;
  hasNext: boolean;
  hasPrev: boolean;
}
```

- **PaginationParams**:
  - Used for sending pagination-related parameters to API endpoints
  - `page` and `limit` determine which page of results to fetch and how many items per page
  - `sort` specifies which field to sort by (optional)
  - `order` defines the sort direction as either ascending or descending (optional)

- **PaginatedResult**:
  - Generic interface for returning paginated data
  - `items` contains the actual data items for the current page
  - `total` represents the total number of items across all pages
  - `page` and `limit` reflect the current pagination settings
  - `pages` indicates the total number of available pages
  - `hasNext` and `hasPrev` are boolean flags indicating if there are more pages in either direction
  - Helps build pagination UI components (page numbers, next/previous buttons)

## Key Benefits of This Type System

1. **Type Safety**: Prevents runtime errors by catching type mismatches during development
2. **Documentation**: These interfaces serve as self-documenting code
3. **Consistency**: Ensures consistent data structures across your application
4. **Code Completion**: Provides IDE autocompletion for these data structures
5. **Maintainability**: Makes refactoring easier as changes to these interfaces will show compilation errors wherever they're used

This type definition file follows best practices by centralizing common types and using TypeScript's advanced features like optional properties, union types, and generics to create a robust type system for your application.

