 No, you wouldn't need to create raw HTML when using Next.js. That's one of the major advantages of using Next.js with React - you work with components rather than raw HTML.

With Next.js and React, you create reusable components using JSX syntax, which looks like HTML but is actually a JavaScript extension that gets compiled into JavaScript code. This component-based approach is much more powerful and developer-friendly than writing raw HTML.

Here's a simple example of what creating a page would look like:

```jsx
// pages/lesson.js or app/lesson/page.js
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import LessonHeader from '../components/LessonHeader';
import WritingPrompt from '../components/WritingPrompt';
import FeedbackSection from '../components/FeedbackSection';

export default function LessonPage({ lessonId }) {
  const [lessonData, setLessonData] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Fetch lesson data from your MongoDB
    fetch(`/api/lessons/${lessonId}`)
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => setLessonData(data));
  }, [lessonId]);

  if (!lessonData) return <div>Loading...</div>;

  return (
    <div className="lesson-container">
      <LessonHeader title={lessonData.title} level={lessonData.level} />
      <WritingPrompt prompt={lessonData.prompt} />
      <FeedbackSection lessonId={lessonId} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

Next.js provides:

1. **Component system** - Build reusable UI elements
2. **Styling options** - CSS modules, Styled Components, Tailwind CSS, etc.
3. **Page routing** - Automatic routing based on file structure
4. **Layout system** - Shared layouts across multiple pages
5. **Image optimization** - Built-in image component for optimization

This approach is much more maintainable and scalable than writing raw HTML. You can build complex interfaces while leveraging React's component reusability and Next.js's powerful features.

